<h2>What happened</h2>
<p>The appeal arose from a matrimonial proceeding under the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, in which allegations of cruelty and desertion were pressed and failed before the Family Court. The Division Bench, after examining the record, found no reason to disturb the trial court&rsquo;s conclusions and dismissed the first appeal.</p>
<p>The judgment&rsquo;s practical significance lies in paragraph 26. Having affirmed the decree, the Bench turned to the wife&rsquo;s maintenance — fixed at Rs 10,000 per month — and held that the figure required revision. Applying the principles in <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, the court enhanced the amount to Rs 15,000 per month and granted liberty to seek modification in the future.</p>

<h2>Why appellate courts revisit maintenance</h2>
<p>Maintenance is not a one-time adjudication. It is a continuing obligation that tracks the parties&rsquo; means and needs over time. Three features of the law make an appellate enhancement like this one possible:</p>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. The affidavit regime.</strong> Since <em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em>, both parties must file affidavits of assets and liabilities in every maintenance proceeding. The disclosed financial picture gives appellate courts a concrete basis to test whether the figure fixed below remains realistic.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Inflation and changed needs.</strong> A figure fixed years earlier — this first appeal dates from 2021 — loses value by efflux of time. Courts routinely account for the erosion of purchasing power when the matter reaches them on appeal.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. The liberty to modify.</strong> Maintenance orders remain open to variation on a change of circumstances. The Bench&rsquo;s express liberty to seek modification keeps both sides&rsquo; remedies intact.</div>
</div>

<h2>The Delhi practice angle</h2>
<p>For litigants in Delhi, the same framework governs maintenance under Section 24 and 25 of the Hindu Marriage Act, Section 125 of the Code of Criminal Procedure — now Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, 2023 — and Section 20 of the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act, 2005. The <em>Rajnesh</em> affidavits are insisted upon in the Family Courts across Delhi, and appellate benches of the High Court of Delhi apply the same criteria: the status of the parties, reasonable needs of the wife and children, the applicant&rsquo;s own income and earning capacity, and the liabilities of the payer.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>Worth remembering:</strong> a party resisting enhancement must do more than point to the original order. The question before an appellate court is whether the figure remains just today — not whether it was just when fixed.</p>
</div>

<h2>Takeaways</h2>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li>Dismissal of the substantive matrimonial appeal does not freeze ancillary orders; maintenance can be recalibrated in the same judgment.</li>
  <li><em>Rajnesh v. Neha</em> remains the operative framework for quantum, disclosure and enforcement across all maintenance statutes.</li>
  <li>Liberty to seek modification is not a formality — it is the mechanism through which maintenance keeps pace with real circumstances, and either side may invoke it.</li>
  <li>Parties should keep their asset-and-liability affidavits current at every stage; stale disclosures invite adverse inferences.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<p>The judgment is a compact illustration of how Indian matrimonial appeals actually conclude: the headline relief may fail, yet the economic terms of the relationship are adjusted to present-day reality. Separately, the same Bench applied these principles in other matrimonial appeals decided the same week, underscoring that maintenance review is now a routine component of first-appeal hearings rather than an afterthought.</p>
